{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1259421-interesting-campaign-city-ideas","id":1259421,"title":"Interesting campaign / city ideas","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-10-07T09:39:56-07:00","id":1259421,"title":"Interesting campaign / city ideas","content":"I placed this in the general WoD section because it could easily be used for any of the games.<br /> <br /> The other day whilst procrastinating I came across a list of the \u201cweirdest small towns in the US\u201d (link at the bottom). There are 16 towns listed, although some are only weird because of odd names or a little oddity. Some, however, have the potential to be unique places to feature in a game.<br /> <br /> I have included my highlights.<br /> <br /> Centralia, PA.<br /> Basically, the town is on top of a coal fire that has been raging for 50 years. No one is allowed to move there but there are still residents that haven't moved out. This screams wyrm to me, although could easily be a demon or something equally terrible. Are the remaining inhabitants in the thrall of the wrym, or are they the defenders / guards keeping something truly horrific trapped.<br /> <br /> Gibsonton, Fl.<br /> A town that has a large seasonal population comprising of circus folk wintering in the sun. I especially liked that the town (according to Wikipedia) has zoning laws that allow residents to keep elephants and circus trailers on the lawns. <br /> <br /> Slab City, CA.<br /> A deserted military base in the California badlands. Occupied by snowbirds, hippies and squatters. Has no electricity, water, government, police etc. Features an open-air nightclub amongst other things.<br /> <br /> Whittier, AK<br /> Population of 214 all live in same building that also houses the school, police etc.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a